Changes made relative to upstream
---------------------------------

Per GPLv3 section 5(a), this notice records that the following significant
changes were made when creating SparkyBin Browser from GNOME Web (Epiphany):

  * Renamed the application and all user-visible branding from
    "GNOME Web" / "Epiphany" to "SparkyBin Browser".
  * Changed the application ID from `org.gnome.Epiphany` to
    `com.sparkybin.Browser`, including all derived D-Bus names, GSettings
    schema IDs and paths, GResource prefixes, and object paths.
  * Changed the executable name from `epiphany` to `sparkybin-browser`.
  * Replaced the application icon artwork with new placeholder artwork.
  * Updated the AppStream metadata, desktop entry, D-Bus service files,
    GSettings schema, Doxygen/DOAP metadata, manual page, help pages, and
    build system to use the new name and identifiers.
  * Changed developer/organization metadata, homepage, and bug tracker URLs.

Behavioural changes
-------------------

  * Enabled WebExtensions by default. The `enable-webextensions` key of the
    `com.sparkybin.Browser.web` GSettings schema defaults to true, so the
    Extensions page is offered in the Preferences out of the box. Upstream
    defaults this to false and treats the feature as experimental; the
    implementation's security posture is unchanged.
  * Moved the default profile, cache, and config directories from
    `~/.local/share/epiphany`, `~/.cache/epiphany`, and `~/.config/epiphany`
    to the application-ID-based paths `~/.local/share/com.sparkybin.Browser`,
    `~/.cache/com.sparkybin.Browser`, and `~/.config/com.sparkybin.Browser`.
    This stops a non-sandboxed installation from sharing user data with a
    GNOME Web installation.
  * Set the program name to `sparkybin-browser` so that GLib and GTK warnings
    and log messages are no longer attributed to `epiphany`.
  * Fixed a number of security, memory-safety, and lifetime defects in the
    WebExtension implementation (use-after-free of extension objects held by
    web views, a JavaScript-injection path in `downloads.search()`, a
    downloads path traversal, injection of the extension API into cross-origin
    frames, and others). See the repository history for details.

